    "summary": "The goal is to \"fully connect 1.25 million more New Yorkers to affordable high-speed internet by 2034, targeting historically underserved neighborhoods where gaps are the greatest. Specifically, the proposal outlines five avenues to closing remaining gaps: increasing broadband competition, expanding access to affordable and free Internet, scaling community Internet in targeted areas, bolstering digital skills, and direct investments in neighborhoods with the greatest gaps. That law, which requires ISPs to provide an affordable monthly plan (between $15-$20) for low-income New Yorkers, was unsuccessfully challenged by the industry in the courts. In the Bronx, where residents face the highest broadband prices (at $75.70/month), the adoption rate is 63%, the lowest of the five boroughs.",
