    "summary": "While SpaceX did not achieve the targeted \"double catch\" that would have returned Starship's lower-stage booster and upper-stage \"Ship\" safely back to Earth, it did retrieve the upper-stage successfully for the first time ever on Saturday. After deploying another 26 high-capacity \"V3\" Starlink satellites and spending nearly a day in orbit, the 171-foot Ship portion deorbited, navigated its way to a tower at Starbase in Texas and then gently maneuvered itself into the awaiting arms of \"Mechazilla\" (SpaceX's launch and landing tower platform) at 4:20 p. Related: Amazon Leo still tracking for 2026 service launch as satellite production ramps The analysts at New Street Research agreed in a research note (registration required) that the \"major hurdles for reusability have passed. SpaceX will now inspect the ship's engines and heat shield to refine future Starship designs.",
