    "publishedAt": "2026-11-16",
    "summary": "T-Mobile must now end data caps and expand offerings or risk penalties. The law, passed in 2021 and enacted in 2025 , requires that Internet service providers offer low-income households a $15/month plan of at least 25 Mbit/s, or a $20/month plan of at least 200 Mbit/s. But according to New York's investigation, while T-Mobile offered a 25Mbit/s fixed wireless plan for $15, the operator was found to implement data caps on customers who adopted it, resulting in \"significantly slower\" download speeds after just a few days of usage. Related: Affordable broadband law could reduce California ISP revenue by 'less than 1%' – study Complying with those measures will absolve T-Mobile in this matter, but the ABA does allow for the imposition of civil penalties up to $1,000 per violation.",
