    "publishedAt": "2026-11-24",
    "summary": "UT Arlington and Intel measured software sync costs on a real four-host shared CXL memory pool, as vendors eye CXL for AI KV cache. AMD shares how it can retain 95% of server performance with just half the DRAM, sidestepping memory shortages with MEXT Penguin shows off photonic memory box at SC26 that can expand AI accelerator capacity over optical links Micron announces 512GB DDR5-9200 memory modules with 16W power draw The result: breadth-first search (BFS), a standard graph algorithm and a common benchmark, “gains nothing from adding a second host,” and was actually slower on four servers than on one, at 0.74s versus 0.55s. Its abstract also claims 3.0x lower P99 latency and 1.9x higher throughput. It claimed 10x to 30x agentic AI gains, with famfs support for apps like Nvidia’s Dynamo.",
